---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/coderturtle/half-life";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const recentEntries = (await getCollection("buildlog", ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Half-Life: guide"
  description="Don't take the paper's word for it - measure your own model's context decay, then prove your fix actually works."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Half-Life</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-lg opacity-80">
    Don't take the paper's word for it - measure your own model's context decay, then prove your
    fix actually works.
  </p>

  <div class="post-body mt-10">
    <p>
      This page is the guide for anyone landing on the deployed site rather than the repo
      directly. The workshop itself, six modules covering context window mechanics through
      mitigation strategies plus a synthesis capstone, lives in the <a href={REPO}>repo</a>, whose
      own README covers the same ground in more detail once you've cloned it. Nothing here
      substitutes for cloning it; this is orientation, not the content.
    </p>

    <h2>Two gates, not one</h2>
    <p>
      Every exercise runs through your own coding-agent harness (Claude Code, Codex, or
      equivalent), scripted and non-interactive. An <strong>empirical gate</strong> checks first:
      a scripted, scored measurement against a stated threshold, no judgment call. Then a{" "}
      <strong>conceptual gate</strong>: Coachgremlin, this workshop's teaching agent (a role you
      run yourself, inside your own harness, not a hosted service), checks whether you designed a
      genuinely single-variable experiment and can explain the result you got. A passing
      measurement is necessary, never sufficient on its own.
    </p>

    <h2>Real research, reproduced by you, not just cited</h2>
    <p>
      This workshop's arc is anchored to real, current material: Lost in the Middle (Liu et al.),
      Greg Kamradt's needle-in-a-haystack methodology, Chroma's Context Rot report, and
      Anthropic's own context-engineering guidance. None of it is hands-on for a practitioner; the
      papers are read, not reproduced. This workshop's bet is that turning "trust the paper" into
      "reproduce it yourself, against your own tool" is a genuine addition. See the repo's{" "}
      <code>docs/workshop-design.md</code> for the full curriculum-anchor research.
    </p>

    <h2>What you walk away with</h2>
    <p>
      Two different things get built during each module: your own judgment, and a reusable
      artifact your agent can load next time. See each module's own README (once authored) for
      the evidence behind its specific gate.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>You</strong> get practiced judgment: reading a measured result and correctly
        explaining why it looks the way it does, designing a genuinely single-variable experiment,
        and knowing how much of a model's context window you can actually trust for a given task.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your agent</strong> gets the packaged takeaways: a context-inspection habit, a
        reusable needle-in-a-haystack test script, a safe-context-budget measurement script, a
        pre/post-compaction recall probe, a mitigation decision guide, and a personal
        context-diagnosis playbook tying it all together.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The runbook</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Prerequisites: comfortable with git, the CLI, and reading a diff; already using a
        coding-agent harness regularly; willing to run scripted, non-interactive harness
        invocations against your own API usage.
      </li>
      <li>
        Clone it: <code>git clone {REPO}.git</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Start at <code>modules/README.md</code>. It has the full arc, in order, and a table of
        what each module's gate requires and what you keep from it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Work through the six modules in order: context window mechanics, positional bias,
        length-driven context rot, compaction mechanics, mitigation strategies, then the synthesis
        capstone. Each states a hard prerequisite on an earlier one.
      </li>
      <li>
        No module completes by reading it. Every module states a required gate, checked first
        empirically, then conceptually. If a module ever reduces to "read this, then move on," or
        a link here is broken, that's a defect: <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  {
    recentEntries.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 class="mt-14 text-xl font-semibold">Recent from the build log</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          {recentEntries.map((entry) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`${base}build-log/${entry.id}/`} class="entry-card">
                <p class="entry-title text-lg font-semibold">{entry.data.title}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm opacity-80">{entry.data.description}</p>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm opacity-70">
          See the <a href={`${base}build-log/`} class="underline">full index</a>.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }
</BaseLayout>
